ing  well mentally  meanwhile the protests continue  just not outdoors  these women have gathered to send a  message to the world  calling for education freedom and access  to work  the only safe place to do it is behind  these walls  and joining me now for more from colombo  is samira hamid the amnesty  international south asia campaigner  samira you're regularly in touch with  women in afghanistan who have been  forced to sit at home because of taliban  dictats do they feel it's safe to go out  and protest for their rights  thank you for having me uh  yes based on the uh  discussions and calls that i regularly  make to um  women and girls in afghanistan  not only the general ones because of my  work but also the female family members  the relatives that i have um it is quite  a  difficult situation right now for women  and girls in afghanistan especially if a  woman  would like to mobilize and uh they would  like to go out on the streets to  for example protest uh  in one hand the taliban have banned  protests  earlier a couple of months ago  in other hands the taliban of course  used violence and uh uh  where they  they did not allow the woman protesters  to continue protests and also what uh  what we have seen the pattern and also  what we have documented in our recent  report is that  a majority of women protesters were  identified disappeared they were  arrested tortured and even they were  threatened to death if they continue uh  so this is uh it's a it's quite a  dangerous  environment right now where uh women are  uh working um however they have not  given up what i am hearing especially  from the woman who are mobilizing and  who are part of the protests from the  past right and that's what we see in the  report that we play uh just before the  conversation with you but it's also  pretty clear that the taliban have  broken their promises on women's rights  the promises that they were making  during the doha round of talks for  instance do you see a situation where  the taliban will restore women's rights  i i don't  i don't think so  i would like to say that when the  taliban took over in august last year  uh you know some of the fancy promises  that they made especially through  international media and in their  meetings with international community  there was a shadow of hope that  i had but unfortunately  the one year of taliban and afghanistan  the way they have dismantled for example  ministry of women's affairs the way they  have  consistently uh they have introduced  different policies and decrease on uh  women's rights on how women should uh  you know live in afghanistan and also  the fact that they have failed to  address uh the issue of girls education  which is a very straightforward thing um  unfortunately does not give me any hope  anymore you know they made these  promises to the international community  and the international community seem to  sort of accept it do you think it is  incumbent upon the international  community to actually force the taliban  to respect the promises that the taliban  themselves made  handed person  uh international community uh plays a  massive role in  where afghanistan is today we  they accept it or they don't accept it  but they are part of the problem where  this crisis happened in afghanistan last  year and  accept  blank promises and statements  international community until now have  not hold the taliban accountable and i  think after giving it giving them a year  uh to actually prove themselves that  they do respect  human rights in general but particularly  women's rights this is time for  international community to take action  this is time for the international  community to use their leverage to hold  the taliban accountable just one final  question ms amity if you're a woman or a  girl in afghanistan today what is the  message that goes out to them what  should they be doing  uh i think they are brave and uh  the fact that they they are showing the  world and t